README.md
   1: # Lyceum
   2: 
   3: **A curriculum-review prototype being developed into a lecturer's course workspace.**
   4: 
   5: The [improvement plan](docs/lyceum-improvement-plan.md) is the current product
   6: direction: traceable industry evidence, reviewed CLO/topic changes, assessments,
   7: editable teaching materials, and versioned academic review. It includes the
   8: repository audit, proposed agent identities, calculation design, source-access
   9: strategy, SDK/subscription options, accessibility requirements, and release gates.
  10: 
  11: The current app supports personal course creation, a complete manual CLO/topic
  12: editor, course constraints, before/after comparisons, local version history,
  13: CLO-linked teaching sessions, Markdown exports and validated JSON backup/restore.
  14: It also retains the prototype's role personas, document parsing and review,
  15: assessment editor, prerequisite checks, lecturer-supplied evidence receipts with
  16: exact excerpts, and illustrative analytics.
  17: It is not yet a validated curriculum-rating or learning-prediction service:
  18: 
  19: - Market scores, source counts and dates are **synthetic fixtures**. There are
  20:   no underlying retrieved postings or live collection connectors in this build.
  21:   The Relevance Index uses fixed demonstration inputs.
  22: - The seeded 200-learner cohort is synthetic. The historical backtest is a
  23:   generated example, and S-0488 / ML-14 is a separately pinned illustration.
  24:   The displayed error of 0.009 is not evidence of predictive accuracy in practice.
  25: - The Rasch-style simulation uses heuristic ability/difficulty/penalty values.
  26:   Topic edits are not a validated model of learning gains.
  27: - Role switching is prototype state. Approval now applies the tested candidate
  28:   to a local version with role and stale-review checks, but server-side identity,
  29:   authorization and institutional verification remain to be built.
  30: - Upload parsing is functional. A successful parse or an uploaded syllabus does
  31:   not establish an official source; confirmation keeps institutional verification unset.
  32: - Live text can augment the agent panel, but many structured drafts and scores
  33:   still use fixtures. Slide generation/export is planned, not implemented.
  34: 
  35: ## Lecturer workspace
  36: 
  37: Start with **Courses → Create course workspace → Outcomes & topics** or open an
  38: assigned course. Record the course brief, CLOs, achievement evidence, PLO mappings
  39: and topic sequence. Declare known hours and prerequisites. Save and check the
  40: revision, inspect its changes and warnings, record a rationale and teaching term,
  41: then explicitly choose **Use as my course version**.
  42: 
  43: That decision applies your entered outline and preserves the baseline and history.
  44: It is local course preparation; official syllabus approval stays in the
  45: university's existing process. Broken links, cycles, exceeded budgets and removed
  46: protected content block a checked revision. Missing information stays visible
  47: instead of becoming an arbitrary readiness score.
  48: 
  49: In **Evidence**, run a research run: fetch postings from a permitted employer
  50: board (Greenhouse or Lever, through the local API) or the dated saved sample,
  51: keep the ones worth reading, scan them for the skills you name, and confirm each
  52: quotation in the source's exact words; a paraphrase is rejected. Mark the
  53: postings reviewed so they count, map quoted skills to outcomes with a reviewed
  54: coverage stage, then prepare a meeting brief with the table, the options and
  55: every receipt, to download or to send to a colleague inside Lyceum. You can also
  56: paste a document you are permitted to keep. Collection happens only when you
  57: ask, and nothing changes the course by itself.
  58: 
  59: In **Teaching plan**, save sessions with CLO links, activities and learning checks,
  60: and download any session as an editable **teaching pack (.pptx)** built from the
  61: session and the evidence linked to its outcomes, with speaker notes and no AI
  62: text. The meeting brief downloads as slides too.
  63: A course change preserves affected teaching work and flags it for review.
  64: Affected assessments cannot be approved again until their course-change review
  65: is recorded and retired outcome links are repaired.
  66: 
  67: Use **Guide & progress → Workspace backup** or **Courses → Backups** to download
  68: saved data and versions as JSON. Restore includes a file preview, validation,
  69: replacement acknowledgement and a downloadable pre-restore recovery copy.
  70: Markdown course/plan exports are readable documents; JSON is the restorable
  71: workspace format. Original uploaded files and unsaved edits are excluded.
  72: 
  73: The core workflow needs **no paid AI subscription or API key**. Work is saved in
  74: this browser only. Shared accounts, server permissions, term archives and
  75: multi-device collaboration remain in development.
  76: 
  77: The **Guide & progress** page explains every section and lists unfinished work.
  78: Every signed-in section also has a **How to use** disclosure. Read the full
  79: [lecturer and section guide](docs/lecturer-guide.md) for instructions, outputs and
  80: limitations. Regenerate it with `node scripts/generate-guide.mjs` when changing
  81: the shared in-app section catalogue.
  82: 
  83: ---
  84: 
  85: ## Screenshots
  86: 
  87: The October lecturer workspace, using an example teaching plan entered during
  88: the browser checks:
  89: 
  90: ![Lecturer home](docs/screenshots/workspace-home.png)
  91: 
  92: ![Teaching plan](docs/screenshots/workspace-teaching-plan.png)
  93: 
  94: ![Reviewed personal course outline](docs/screenshots/workspace-curriculum.png)
  95: 
  96: The remaining screenshots document the original v3 interface and predate the
  97: October integrity and workspace improvements. Current screens explicitly label
  98: the synthetic data.
  99: 
 100: **Trends.** The Signal example maps synthetic market figures onto course outcomes.
 101: 
 102: ![Trends](docs/screenshots/10-trends.png)
 103: 
 104: **Real intake.** What was read beside what the agent made of it; nothing files
 105: until a human confirms.
 106: 
 107: ![Upload review](docs/screenshots/20-upload-review.png)
 108: 
 109: **The public Relevance Index.** An illustrative rating view, accessible without signing in.
 110: 
 111: ![Relevance Index](docs/screenshots/02-index-public.png)
 112: 
